@page
@model Web.Pages.HowItWorksModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "How the Predictions Work";
    ViewData["HideBackground"] = true;
    var test = Model.Info.Test;
    var gain = Model.Comparison("vs before fight stats", "accuracy");
    var overRule = Model.Comparison("vs better win rate", "accuracy");
    var others = test.Families.Keys.Where(f => f != "logistic regression").ToList();
    bool anotherIsBetter = others.Any(f => Model.Comparison($"{f} vs logistic regression", "log_loss") is { } c && c[2] < 0);
}

<article class="prose">
    <div class="fighter-header">
        <h1>How the predictions work</h1>
        <p class="fighter-meta">A logistic regression on each fighter's UFC record and fight stats, tested on fights it never saw.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="stats-section-title">How well it does</h2>
    <p>
        @if (Model.Info.TrainedSince is DateTime since)
        {
            <text>The model learned from UFC fights from @since.Year until @test.From.ToString("MMMM yyyy") (each fighter's
            history still goes back to UFC 1)</text>
        }
        else
        {
            <text>The model was trained on every UFC fight before @test.From.ToString("MMMM yyyy")</text>
        }
        and then asked to pick the @test.Model.Bouts.ToString("N0") fights from @test.From.ToString("MMM yyyy") to
        @test.To.ToString("MMM yyyy"), using only what was known the day before each one.
    </p>
    <div class="table-card section-card">
        <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Picks the winner</th><th>Log loss</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>This model</strong></td><td><strong>@HowItWorksModel.Percent(test.Model.Accuracy)</strong></td><td><strong>@test.Model.LogLoss?.ToString("0.000")</strong></td></tr>
                <tr><td>The model before fight stats (record, Elo, and finishing)</td><td>@HowItWorksModel.Percent(test.BeforeFightStats.Accuracy)</td><td>@test.BeforeFightStats.LogLoss?.ToString("0.000")</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Whoever has the better UFC win rate</td><td>@HowItWorksModel.Percent(test.BetterWinRate.Accuracy)</td><td>&ndash;</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Whoever has the higher Elo rating</td><td>@HowItWorksModel.Percent(test.EloOnly.Accuracy)</td><td>@test.EloOnly.LogLoss?.ToString("0.000")</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Coin flip</td><td>50.0%</td><td>0.693</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    @if (gain != null && overRule != null)
    {
        <p>
            Adding each fighter's striking and grappling history@(Model.Info.TrainedSince is DateTime s
                ? $", and learning only from fights since {s.Year},"
                : "") made the difference. The model picks
            @HowItWorksModel.Points(gain[0]) percentage points more winners than it did before
            (95% interval @HowItWorksModel.Points(gain[1]) to @HowItWorksModel.Points(gain[2])), and
            @HowItWorksModel.Points(overRule[0]) points more than simply backing the fighter with the better record
            (@HowItWorksModel.Points(overRule[1]) to @HowItWorksModel.Points(overRule[2])).
            @(gain[1] > 0 && overRule[1] > 0
                ? "Both gains are bigger than chance could produce on this many fights."
                : gain[1] > 0
                    ? "The gain over the old model is bigger than chance; the edge over the simple rule is within what chance could produce on this many fights."
                    : "Neither gain is bigger than what chance could produce on this many fights.")
        </p>
    }
    <p>
        Log loss rewards being confident when right and punishes being confident when wrong (lower is better).
        @(Model.CalibrationGap > 0.015
            ? "The percentages are a little cautious: the favorite wins somewhat more often than the model says."
            : Model.CalibrationGap < -0.015
                ? "The percentages are a little bold: the favorite wins somewhat less often than the model says."
                : "The percentages mean what they say:")
    </p>
    <div class="table-card section-card calibration">
        @foreach (var bucket in test.Calibration)
        {
            <div class="calibration-row">
                <span class="calibration-label">Favorite given @((bucket.From * 100).ToString("0"))&ndash;@((bucket.To * 100).ToString("0"))%</span>
                <div class="calibration-bars">
                    <div class="calibration-bar predicted" style="width: @((bucket.Predicted * 100).ToString("0"))%"><span>said @((bucket.Predicted * 100).ToString("0"))%</span></div>
                    <div class="calibration-bar actual" style="width: @((bucket.Actual * 100).ToString("0"))%"><span>won @((bucket.Actual * 100).ToString("0"))%</span></div>
                </div>
                <span class="calibration-count">@bucket.Bouts fights</span>
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    <h2 class="stats-section-title">What goes into a prediction</h2>
    <p>Everything comes from the two fighters' UFC fights before the one being predicted:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Elo rating</strong>: a rating that rises with wins and falls with losses, by more when the result is an upset.</li>
        <li><strong>Record and form</strong>: UFC win rate, the last three results, and the current win or losing streak.</li>
        <li><strong>Finishing</strong>: knockouts and submissions per fight, and how often the fighter has been finished.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Striking</strong>: significant strikes landed and absorbed per minute, striking accuracy and defense,
            and knockdowns per 15 minutes.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Grappling</strong>: takedowns per 15 minutes, takedown accuracy and defense, time spent in control
            minus time controlled, and submission attempts.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Age</strong> on the day of the fight. Other things equal, the younger fighter is favored.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>UFC experience</strong>: number of UFC fights. Once record is accounted for, more fights counts slightly
            <em>against</em> a fighter, probably because long careers mean older fighters.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Time since last fight</strong>. A longer gap counts slightly in a fighter's favor, probably because
            fighters on a quick turnaround are often short-notice replacements.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The striking and grappling numbers are built fight by fight from
        <a href="https://github.com/Greco1899/scrape_ufc_stats">a public export of ufcstats.com's per-fight stats</a>,
        so each prediction only sees the fights that came before it. A fighter with little UFC fight time is pulled toward
        the league average until there's enough to go on, and fighters making their UFC debut are treated as average.
        Dream fights use each fighter as of their last UFC fight.
    </p>

    <h2 class="stats-section-title">Three kinds of model</h2>
    <p>
        A gradient-boosted tree model and a small neural network were given the same fights and the same choices of
        features and training years, and chosen and tested the same way:
    </p>
    <div class="table-card section-card">
        <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Picks the winner</th><th>Log loss</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var (family, score) in test.Families)
                {
                    <tr><td>@(char.ToUpper(family[0]) + family[1..])</td><td>@HowItWorksModel.Percent(score.Accuracy)</td><td>@score.LogLoss?.ToString("0.000")</td></tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        @{
            var verdicts = others
                .Select(f => (Family: f, Interval: Model.Comparison($"{f} vs logistic regression", "log_loss")))
                .Where(v => v.Interval != null)
                .Select(v => (v.Family, Verdict: HowItWorksModel.LogLossVerdict(v.Interval!)))
                .ToList();
        }
        @if (verdicts.Count > 1 && verdicts.All(v => v.Verdict == verdicts[0].Verdict))
        {
            <text>On log loss, both were @verdicts[0].Verdict the logistic regression. </text>
        }
        else
        {
            foreach (var v in verdicts)
            {
                <text>On log loss, @v.Family was @v.Verdict the logistic regression. </text>
            }
        }
        @(anotherIsBetter
            ? "The site still uses the logistic regression for now, because its reasons for each pick can be shown exactly."
            : "Neither did better, so the site uses the logistic regression, whose reasons for each pick can be shown exactly. With a few thousand fights to learn from, the extra flexibility probably has too little to work with.")
    </p>

    <h2 class="stats-section-title">What was tried and left out</h2>
    <ul>
        @if (Model.Info.TrainedSince is DateTime firstYear)
        {
            <li>
                <strong>Learning from fights before @firstYear.Year</strong> made predictions worse on the years held out
                for choosing the model, probably because the sport has changed so much since its early days. Those fights
                still count toward each fighter's record, Elo rating, and stats.
            </li>
        }
        <li>
            <strong>Height, reach, and stance</strong> made predictions worse on the years held out for choosing the model.
            Many reaches are missing from the data.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Matchup terms</strong>, one fighter's striking or takedowns against the other's defense, didn't help either,
            probably because the individual stats already carry most of that information.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>UFC.com's career stats</strong>, such as strikes landed per minute, are current totals that include the
            very fights being predicted, so using them would leak the results. The model rebuilds them fight by fight instead.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Betting odds</strong> aren't used. Odds reflect things no record shows, like injuries and short-notice
            replacements, so a bookmaker's favorite is still a stronger pick than this model's.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="stats-section-title">Cleaning the data first</h2>
    <p>
        The first version of the model picked 71% of winners, which was too good to be true. Duplicates in the data were
        leaking results:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            The API lists most recent events twice under different addresses (for example "UFC 322" and "UFC 322: Della
            Maddalena vs. Makhachev"), so a fight's own result showed up in the history used to predict its twin. The scraper
            now marks these, and the site and model skip them.
        </li>
        <li>Some fights appear twice on one card, or on an unrelated later card. These are marked the same way.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The API also lists the loser first in almost every fight, which a model could learn instead of anything real, so
        each fight is used both ways round in training.
    </p>
    <p class="fighter-meta">
        Trained on @Model.Info.TrainingBouts.ToString("N0") fights (@Model.Info.TrainedOn). Elo K-factor @Model.Info.EloK.
    </p>
</article>
